 conversation we had  with the Turkish mother child foundation  who have implemented mother support  programs workshops that are geared  towards mothers and their children for  25 years in in Turkey and a program  that's implemented a national level and  they have just recently launched a  father support program because the  Turkish mothers came to the organization  and said look we can't implement  everything that you've taught us at home  with our children if the men are going  to be obstacles at home so please bring  them on board so they had this father  support group which is originally  Auxiliary and then they discovered that  fathers really really matter and so I  think they were the one who made a  stumble upon the importance of fathers  but we were the one who set that  evidence on a scientific global  perspective okay so let's talk about the  methodology of your work so the  methodology was a systematic review of  the literature so we really had to do  two kinds of searches one of them is the  very standard literature search over at  what time period just one year okay I  mean I mean we took a year but the their  parenting interventions that those that  exist forever okay and that captures the  very best known interventions in the  world today and then we did a second  search automatic search to get to the  most to the ones that don't don't get  published in the literature and  discovered parenting interventions and  places like Ukraine pyronesia Jordan  Pakistan and other places how did you  get at that secondary level aspesi be  difficult to go through contacts through  websites 31 a good example of the UNICEF  programs that are rolled out across the  world  so through a detective work that's  two-pronged approach between the  systemic and the sleuth right finding  put those articles together so in the  end we ended up with a thousand articles  that were parenting interventions with  dads on board not just mothering  interventions and we just in about 20  different countries and we looked at  those pretty closely okay so what do you  think the implications of this study are  well for for one it's it's not a very  good idea to sidelined importance of  fathers and for a second we need a  policy change at the highest level in  terms of the design of our interventions  that can sideline them so we have no  means of really effectively evaluating  or engaging with men and it's not that  we want to just engage with men as  opposed to mothers but we want to  leverage co-parenting if possible and we  want to find out find out what works  best in particularly particular context  so to give you an example my favorite  example is the in nazare equalled amake  which means school for husbands which  will men joined when they get married in  order to get schooled into the skills of  being fathers and husbands and they  learn about gender equity and their  power to to give children access to  education and care so of course many  other decision-makers so the stakes are  very high but it's a lovely way of  bringing on board the men now the good  example in Brazil is the work from  instituto profundo bro mundo that looks  at ways of redefining the masculinity of  men so that they move away from a  disposition of violence and domestic  violence inside their home and really  become effective caregivers and more  have more equitable relationships with  their partner so we there are  interventions that really demonstrate  that fatherhood is a transformative  moment for men that they want to raise  their children in a different way and we  can move them away from that disposition  of violence harsh parenting disciplining  masculinity  a world view towards one that makes them  really engage in raising the Generation  Next Generation 44 empathetic and  peaceful more peaceful young men and  women which we think is very important  of course it sounds you know very  idealistic and I wonder you know in  thinking of certain countries it must be  very difficult to institute those kinds  of changes in certain places in the  world that have from a cultural  perspective men being very you know the  ruler yeah the macho right but but you  can you mean how do you work around them  that's what a very interesting way of  doing things that we don't know it fits  the best but this is one that seems to  prove good in certain respects is a  men-only workshop where instead of  bringing women and then together you  give a platform for men to discuss these  issues and they're quite surprised to  find that other men have these kinds of  problems etcetera and find their  strategies find such as used to move  forward so yes things are very difficult  a great example would be Northern  Ireland for peacebuilding in terms of  bringing together growth of Catholics  and Protestants that are coming to the  table with the same idea which is that  their children should not go through  that and they have to raise that  children in a different way and so these  sometimes these men men only group or  even co-parent groups you you learn to  overcome the stereotype that you might  have towards the other which is why we  certainly think that men only group or  co-parent group I'd be more effective  than just home visits because they make  you relate to other people across the  ethnic religious or social divide and  that has the potential for breaking down  some stereotypes and we do find that in  certain places in the world and you know  you have to have the same aspiration for  your children and that's a very good  pathway so we have a book coming out  myself Jim lachman and remus all at  UNICEF called pathway  two piece where we put together this  idea or review the concrete evidence  that we go from the biology of the brain  to peacebuilding in ways that the you  know the standard idea is that the way  we raise our children will matter for  Health and Social Development now the  big new idea the bold idea is that the  way we reserve 